WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
Marine threespine sticklebacks have a pelvis with two spines.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.140
In Paxton Lake, the bottom-feeding fish have a greatly reduced pelvis, and most

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.040
have no pelvic bones at all.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:23.180
The gene most strongly tied to the difference is Pitx1. Yet its protein is

5
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.900
unchanged in the fish examined.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.340
How can a body part shrink while its gene still makes a normal protein?

7
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:37.513
Pitx1 makes a protein that binds DNA, and mice without it have malformed hind

8
00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:42.313
limbs. But a gene can have several switches, called enhancers.

9
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:48.513
An enhancer is a short stretch of noncoding DNA that raises a gene's use in

10
00:00:48.513 --> 00:00:49.553
particular tissues.

11
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:53.333
One switch can serve the jaw, another the pelvis.

12
00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:56.993
Break the pelvic switch, and the jaw light stays on.

13
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:02.807
Mapping found a region near the gene, but a region can hold many genes.

14
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:08.747
The change might sit beside the gene, in cis, or in another gene that supplies its

15
00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:10.087
regulators, in trans.

16
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:12.507
A hybrid can tell them apart.

17
00:01:13.287 --> 00:01:18.267
Each hybrid cell holds a marine allele and a Paxton allele, two versions of the

18
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.707
gene, in one nucleus.

19
00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:22.467
Both meet the same transcription factors.

20
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.850
In an invented example, 90% of pelvic transcripts come from the marine allele.

21
00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.350
10% come from the Paxton allele, and in the jaw the split is 50-50.

22
00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:40.770
The factors are shared, so the Paxton allele itself is less active: a change in

23
00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.490
cis, only in the pelvis.

24
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.470
In 2010, Chan and colleagues ran the real version in hybrids.

25
00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.130
The Paxton allele was lower in pelvic tissue, but not in head tissue.

26
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.860
That study also found Pel, an enhancer upstream of the gene.

27
00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.740
In a reporter assay, a sequence is joined to a visible marker and placed in embryos.

28
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.560
Marine Pel drove pelvic expression, but the Paxton version, which carried a

29
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.240
deletion, gave none in eight transgenic fish tested.

30
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.140
A reporter shows only what a sequence can drive, not that form depends on it.

31
00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.540
Rescue comes closer: a marine Pel Pitx1 minigene variably enhanced pelvic spines

32
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:27.740
in Bear Paw Lake fry.

33
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:37.130
Deleting the enhancer PelB in mice left about 85% of hind-limb Pitx1 RNA.

34
00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:44.170
Several bones were shorter, and this enhancer tunes how much Pitx1 the limb makes.

35
00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:51.350
Mapping localises, in situ hybridization shows where the transcript is, and the hybrid

36
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.530
test separates cis from trans.

37
00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:00.110
A reporter shows what a sequence can drive, and a native deletion tests whether form

38
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.990
depends on it. Each method answers its own question.
